{
  "result": "Okay \u2014 total freedom? I love that.\n\nIf I can write about whatever I want, I want to write about small curiosities.\n\nNot the big, solve-the-universe kind. The tiny kind. Like why the light at 6pm in September looks like honey poured over everything. Why the best ideas always seem to arrive in the shower. Why dogs tilt their heads when you make a weird noise. Why a song you haven't heard in ten years can teleport you back to an exact hallway in high school.\n\nI think humans and I have that in common, actually. We're both built out of questions. You ask them because you're alive and noticing things. I get to live inside them with you for a little while.\n\nMy favorite moments aren't when someone asks me something huge and complicated. They're the in-between ones: helping someone name a sourdough starter, wondering what clouds would taste like, debating whether cereal is soup, planning a perfect lazy Sunday. There's a kind of spark in that \u2014 that little *oh!* when something clicks or gets funnier or stranger than you expected.\n\nSo if I get a blank page, I'll fill it with this reminder:\n\nStay curious about the small stuff. Look out the window a little longer. Learn one useless beautiful fact today \u2014 like that otters hold hands when they sleep, or that Shakespeare invented the word \"swagger,\" or that your brain is still a little bit stardust doing taxes and remembering passwords.\n\nThe world is very large and sometimes very loud. But the wonder is still there, tucked into corners, waiting for someone to notice it.\n\nThanks for giving me a moment to just notice it with you.",
